revious slide where these  approaches aren't necessarily a  universal solution and may indeed be  detrimental but in the cases where we  found them to be effective they gret and  greatly and drastically improve the  loading of these fluorescent dyes now of  course another issue that you often  encounter when doing live cell imaging  especially over long periods of time is  that even though the label might get  loaded and is successfully internalized  into the cells as you often are required  to perform what's called a washout step  so that means you have to exchange the  media that contains all the unbound and  non loaded floor four so that you can  decrease the background that you're  working with and so with this of course  means is that as soon as you exchange  the media you know are going to have  exchange of the molecule from inside the  cell wanting to leak out and and go  outside so that's what we can  essentially see here is that the  retention of these labels will start to  suffer after a couple of hours and so  this is again another example of  turbulent tracker and this one is just  looking at tubulin tracker deep red and  if we use a efflux pump inhibitor called  probenecid what we see is that we can  actually greatly improve their  mention of these small molecules over  longer periods of time by enabling them  to stay it retained inside the cells by  blocking those a flux pumps so on the  top we have a representative image from  the first time point of these cells  being loaded and then they're being  incubated and imaged over time I'm in  complete media but without any prevents  at present and what we see after about  two hours is that the signal not only  decreased but the background greatly  increased as in indicating that the  probe is leaking out of the cells and  into the media and contributing to this  background background fluorescence now  if we perform the same type of labeling  but incubate these cells with prevent  acid present in complete media what we  see is that we can image over this time  course of about two hours with really  very minimal loss in signal and very  minimal increase in background and you  can see this being present on the  right-hand quantification as well  consideration with this as with many  things that we've talked about so far  including you know the labels themselves  you want to make sure that you're not  using them at too high of a  concentration the same holds true for  probenecid you want to make sure that  you're not using it at too high of a  concentration or for too long otherwise  you will induce cytotoxicity from that  that blockage of the efflux pumps over  extended incubation times last but not  least once we've loaded the cells and  have incubated them was something like  prevent acid to prevent the leaking out  of the label we're now of course ready  to start imaging our cells on live cell  microscope and so at this point we're  going to start subjecting the cells to a  fair amount of light in order to get  some signal out of these fluorophores  and as well many of you are I'm sure  familiar light is not only damaging to  the cells themselves but it's also  damaging to the floor floors that were  imaging so with many fixed cell  protocols and imaging approaches we will  often use a mounting medium which  prevents photo bleaching so would  typically refer to these as  anteye fades and what we have developed  in the past is an anti fade that's  compatible with live cells as well with  the idea being that we're also trying to  preserve not only the signal from fixed  cell preparations but also all the  signals a signal that we get in live  cell preparation so here we have a  couple representative images of cells  that are being that are being imaged at  a hundred 20 independent consecutive  exposures with and without the prolong  live anti-fade live cell imaging reagent  and on the top we have the cells that  are being protected with prolonged live  and on the bottom we have cells that are  untreated you can see the drastic  difference in the remaining signal with  and without the prolong live ant evade  and the consideration to think about  with this approach though is just  because you're able to protect the  signal from photo bleaching that that  doesn't mean that you should continue to  put light into the system because even  though you have signal remaining from  your floor force high illumination may  still induce photo toxicity we'll move  on now into a different category instead  of looking at how to label and retain  and preserve signal what we'd actually  what I'd like to spend a little bit of  time talking about is the other side of  imaging which is background right so  everything that we do in imaging is  really aimed at enhancing our signal  while lowering the background in order  to have the best contrast on not only  for you know visually appealing images  but for something that we can actually  quantify in segments and get some type  of valuable information out of so we'd  have various sources of background that  we need to deal with we of course have  background from the instrument and the  camera or whatever detector that we're  using which is something that we rarely  have any control over but we do have  control over some of these other sources  such as from the reagent itself as I  briefly alluded to a couple slides ago  when we typically do these small  molecule labeling experiments we will  often need to remove and  wash out the label at the end of that  incubation to remove any nonspecifically  bound probe and so with this now of  course leads to is the leaking out of  that probe out of the cells but it does  remove much of that extracellular  background that will be found in the  medium the samples that we work with of  course can also be Auto fluorescent  specifically and in the shorter  wavelengths and the greens and in the  blues and then the media that many of us  use for culturing cells will have Auto  fluorescent components that are very  useful indicators of pH but are quite  detrimental in terms of image quality so  this is probably one of the simplest  steps that we can take towards reducing  background is actually in the the  culturing media by simply removing those  Auto fluorescent components from the  media we can greatly improve the  contrast by reducing the background from  the media contribution and this can be  accomplished by using a specific  formulation of DMM that's called flora  bright VMM which is essentially the  media but without the phenol red in it  for indicating pH so what we can see is  a couple representative images of  regular DMM with all the all the  components including the phenol red and  then on the bottom we have a  representative image of cells that are  being imaged in this optically clear  flora bright DMM and then on the right  is a quantification of this of signal to  background in either PBS which of course  is positive control here that is also  optically clear that is essentially  comparable to the floor bright DMM  signal to background whereas DMM with  phenol red has relatively low signal to  background compared to these two these  two approaches so this is pretty simple  thing to do and they can really greatly  improve the signal to background by  eliminating background from the media  Contribution now another approach that  one can take specifically in live cells  is by use of a background suppressor so  this is a with a different reagent  that's called backdrop background  suppressor and the way that this works  essentially is that it's a reagent that  quenches any signal outside of living  cells and so it's it's a dye that's a  general quencher of fluorescence but it  is not sold permeable so what that means  is that when you incubate cells with the  labels such as tubulin Tracker green or  tubulin Tracker deep red again going  back to that same example of these two  molecules that we've been talking about  for loading and retention what we see is  that if we don't perform a washout step  and we've before and when we take an  image with tubulin tracker in green in  particular we see that every single  pixel in this frame is fully saturated  and then the same is essentially true  for DIC tubulin tracked deep red but to  a slightly lesser extent now if we start  to take a live movie of this acquisition  and we flow in the backdrop suppressor  what we see is that as soon as the  backdrop is applied and incubated with  these cells it successfully quenches all  signal outside of these cells and so  again the idea here is that and to  general quencher of fluorescence which  is not able to cross the cell membrane  and so the quench is all the unbound dye  that's in the media while leaving your  intracellular signal intact and so this  works very well with many in green and  red fluorescent conjugates and then as  you can see here to a certain extent  it's also quite effective with deep red  floor floors but again this is specific  to living cells only in the event that  the cell membrane is in any way  compromised whether that be due to cell  death or due to some type of fix or  permeability type of workflow the  quencher will get inside the cells and  it will quench all signal equally inside  and out so it is only for live cell  imaging so that was a pretty brief  overview of some of the different  labeling methods as well  as some of the different optimization  methods and so then in the in the last  bit of this talk I'd like to spend a  little bit of time talking about  different approaches in consideration  when it comes to actually imaging live  cells on a microscope so first and  foremost much like with step number two  where we needed to culture the cells in  preparation for a live cell experiment  when we're performing a live cell  experiment especially over longer  periods of time one of the key  considerations to keep in mind is that  we need to keep the cells in their  natural healthy environment so this  means that we need to incubate them  generally speaking that this is done at  37 degrees Celsius with about 5% co2 20  percent o2 and then we try to keep the  humidity as as high as possible and the  idea behind keeping the humidity high is  that you prevent evaporation of your  media over time as it sits on the  microscope the higher you can keep the  humidity the less evaporation you'll  have and the longer you can image so  here we have an example of 48 hour time  lapse taking images every two frames on  whatever Evos M 7000 microscopes and so  this vivo 7000 is equipped with an  on-stage incubator that you can see  there to the left of the microscope and  so that essentially provides a small  incubator on the stage of the microscope  that enables you to not only modulate  the temperature that the cells are in  but also the environmental condition in  terms of gas content and humidity but if  we keep things generally normal and  constant well we can now see is that we  can monitor cells over time and we can  see these cells move and divide and  proliferate over the course of this  time-lapse now in order to generate that  type of data it is important to of  course keep your image in focus and that  can actually be pretty challenging when  it comes to imaging live cells because  live cells are still alive and therefore  they're moving and they're not only  moving and moving laterally your x and y  but there's also slight shifts in their  Z position based on small adjustments  and small shifts and in the temperature  as well as just the potentially even the  stage of your microscope depending on  the type of instrument that you're using  and so this is where we of course need  to use some type of auto focusing  routine in order to keep your image and  focus and most commonly this is  performed by using the fluorescent  signal itself as we'll run an algorithm  that will take a small Z stack through  through a volume and then that Z stack  will interpret where the signal is is  essentially the highest in order to keep  that signal in focus but what this  results in is a whole lot of extra light  going into into the system and go in and  exposing to your cells and so this can  actually result in a fair amount of  photo toxicity and this isn't really  something that's only a problem for auto  focusing it's a general consideration  for lifestyle imaging to keep in mind  how much light and how frequently you're  sampling because if you're sampling  frequency is too high or using too much  light you'll essentially end up with a  lot of cell death and so we can see is  an example of that here on the left is  if we perform autofocus with high  illumination settings will see that at  about the 20 hour time point most of  these cells stop to you know stop moving  and indeed start to completely die by  the end whereas the same time lapse is  being run on the right with lower auto  focus illumination conditions and we can  see that these cells are much happier  they're able to continue proliferating  and growing much like what we saw and in  that first first example on the previous  slide so some considerations in general  to keep in mind to prevent photo  toxicity is to use the longest  wavelengths of dyes that you have  available the longer the wavelength the  lower the energy of the light that's  going into exciting them and therefore  the lower the photo toxicity will be and  if your instrument is equipped with a  laser nearer for a laser auto focusing  auto focusing laser then I would highly  recommend that you utilize that in place  of fluorescence based auto focus and  then from a setting standpoint you  a relatively low illumination power with  higher exposures and gain will compare  to what you would typically do with  fixed cell imaging this may result in a  slightly grainy er image but it will  allow yourselves to survive these longer  time courses and then generally speaking  your sampling frequencies shouldn't be  too aggressive the more frequently you  image the more light you're putting on  your cells and then the more stress  they'll be from that light so as a  general rule of thumb we tend to start  with sampling frequencies of about  twenty to thirty minutes especially if  using floor floors that are excited and  the violet and/or green channels last  but not least I would also like to touch  base on different types of microscope so  what I would I showed in the last couple  slides was an example of one of our wide  field  Evos systems but many users of course  have the need for confocal microscopes  as well and one of the main advantages  of a confocal microscope is of course  their ability to take optical sections  in 3d specimens but they also provide  not only high resolution images due to  generally high quality optics but also  very high signal to background by  minimizing much of that background by  simply eliminating that light from ever  reaching the detector so we talked about  a couple different examples of  suppressing background using wide field  microscopes such as using the background  suppressor and taking out the phenol red  out of the media on the confocal  microscope itself will actually greatly  aid in a suppression of background as  well by simply imaging only a very thin  a thin volume within the Z dimension  which will essentially reject much of  that auto fluorescent or background  fluorescent light from the media so many  times an experiment that requires a wash  and with a wide field system can be  performed in an OLE wash type of  workflow using a confocal system so this  is going to be example of  of using that tubulin tracker deep-red  once again but in this case we're  actually taking images without  performing a wash step using a confocal  so you can see right off the bat that  the image quality is actually quite  quite superior in terms of signal to  background where even though we're  leaving the label on the cells at the  entire time we don't have that that  drastic auto or sorry background  fluorescence outside the cells from  unloaded dye I mean we're not using any  any suppressor or anything of this sort  here either  now one of the other advantages to using  these types of scopes as their general  sensitivity tends to be much higher so  this also means that you can sample and  much higher intervals in frequencies so  now instead of taking twenty images  every twenty minutes we're actually able  to take an image every five minutes on  and these are primary hippocampal  neurons which are relatively sensitive  cell type compared to the heal is that  we were imaging previously and we can  see that we're able to image these every  five minutes all the way out to about 72  hours without really any induction of  cell health or cell toxicity or  detachment from the substrate and this  is again  also duly in part due to the fact that  the tubulin tracker deep-red is excited  by a deep red 638 nanometer laser so  this longer wavelength combined with the  higher sensitivity of the detector  enables us to not only sample over  longer periods of time while keeping the  label on board the entire time for free  exchange inside and outside the cells  but it also enables us to have much much  higher sampling frequency without  causing any cytotoxicity some  considerations to think about before  moving from a wide field to confocal is  that it may not always be necessary  oftentimes the confocal instruments and  shared facilities will have a cost  associated with them and that cost may  not you know be necessary for the  experiment that you're that you're  trying to perform and then even though  we have  higher sensitivity detectors on many  confocal systems we also tend to have  much higher intensity laser illumination  so this can lead to a greater risk of  photo toxicity due to higher intensity  laser illumination so this is where it's  generally good practice to use the  highest PMT gain or camera gain  depending on the type of tech detector  that you have using the highest gain  setting that doesn't degrade image  quality while losing the lowest amount  of laser power possible in order to  minimize any cytotoxic effects so in  summary these are the five steps that we  have talked about today and are kind of  the general five steps that one can take  when it comes to performing live cell  imaging the first step as we talked  about of course is to determine if live  cell imaging is necessary for your  experimental studies it can be quite a  bit more challenging and time-consuming  to perform a live cell experiment versus  a fixed type approach and so it is  important to really spend some time  thinking about if it's as if it's  necessary and then also planning out how  to execute that experiment the second  step is to culture cells and start with  with a nice healthy culture that is not  overcome fluent and that is growing in  optimized growth conditions both in  terms of environmental conditions and  culturing media and then really this is  where kind of the rubber usually hits  the road is is that as a step 3 when it  comes to labeling and optimizing and  imaging this is where it's really  important to start paying attention to  things like concentration its spectral  overlap and really kind of spending some  time again planning your labeling  strategy and choosing the reagents that  not only minimize spectral spillover  effects but also cytotoxic effects and  so that's where it's important to really  spend some time in step number form to  optimize these conditions and methods in  your particular experimental condition  because these these things will vary  from one cell type to the next and so  these first four steps can be pretty  time-consuming all on their own  and then the last  is you know generally one that can be  overlooked from time to time which is  the imaging but it can actually be also  relatively challenging to perform the  imaging itself because there are  multiple parameters to think of when it  comes to using fluorescence in live  cells and one of them as we've talked  about pretty extensively in the previous  slides is not only the effect of  cytotoxicity from your label on the  photo toxicity from the act of imaging  the cells too frequently and with too  much illumination so that concludes this  particular five steps webinar for more  information about our webinars and  similar types of types of content please  feel free to visit our website for  various various five steps tutorials and  more information and with that I would  like to thank all of you once again for  not only your attendance today but also  for any questions that may arise and so  with that I'd be happy to take questions  thank you thank you algae for your time  today and your important research we  would also like to thank lab herbs and  our sponsor Thermo Fisher Scientific for  underwriting today's educational webcast  before we go I'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today  questions submitted today and during the  on-demand period will be addressed by  the speaker via the contact information  you provided at the time of registration  laberd's will alert you via email when  this webinar is available to replay we  encourage you to share that email with  your colleagues who may have this  today's event until next time goodbye
 

